# Section 3: Step-by-Step Derivations & Analytical Matrix

## 3.1 The Complete C Implementation (Algorithm Walkthrough)

Below is the **full, executable C program** that satisfies the KTU 2024 lab record requirements. Every line is annotated for the analytical matrix.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

/* ===========================================================
 *  PageNode : Doubly Linked List node for browser history
 * =========================================================== */
typedef struct PageNode {
    char url[100];
    struct PageNode *prev;
    struct PageNode *next;
} PageNode;

/* Global head and current pointers */
PageNode *head    = NULL;
PageNode *current = NULL;

/* ---------- Helper: Create and initialize a new node ---------- */
PageNode* createNode(char *url) {
    PageNode *newNode = (PageNode *)malloc(sizeof(PageNode));
    if (newNode == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
        exit(1);
    }
    strcpy(newNode->url, url);
    newNode->prev = NULL;
    newNode->next = NULL;
    return newNode;
}

/* ---------- Operation 1: Visit a new page ---------- */
void visitPage(char *url) {
    /* Step 1: Invalidate forward history if any */
    if (current != NULL && current->next != NULL) {
        PageNode *temp = current->next;
        while (temp != NULL) {
            PageNode *toDelete = temp;
            temp = temp->next;
            free(toDelete);
        }
        current->next = NULL;   /* Sever forward link */
    }

    /* Step 2: Create the new node */
    PageNode *newNode = createNode(url);

    /* Step 3: Insert at the appropriate position */
    if (head == NULL) {
        head = newNode;
    } else {
        newNode->prev = current;
        current->next = newNode;
    }
    current = newNode;          /* Move current to new page */

    printf("Visited: %s\n", current->url);
}

/* ---------- Operation 2: Go back ---------- */
void goBack() {
    if (current == NULL || current->prev == NULL) {
        printf("No previous page in history.\n");
        return;
    }
    current = current->prev;
    printf("Navigated BACK to: %s\n", current->url);
}

/* ---------- Operation 3: Go forward ---------- */
void goForward() {
    if (current == NULL || current->next == NULL) {
        printf("No forward page in history.\n");
        return;
    }
    current = current->next;
    printf("Navigated FORWARD to: %s\n", current->url);
}

/* ---------- Operation 4: Display full history ---------- */
void displayHistory() {
    if (head == NULL) {
        printf("No pages visited yet.\n");
        return;
    }
    PageNode *temp = head;
    int idx = 1;
    printf("\n------- Browser History -------\n");
    while (temp != NULL) {
        if (temp == current) {
            printf("-> [CURRENT] %d. %s\n", idx, temp->url);
        } else {
            printf("   %2d. %s\n", idx, temp->url);
        }
        temp = temp->next;
        idx++;
    }
    printf("-------------------------------\n");
}

/* ---------- Cleanup: free all allocated memory ---------- */
void freeAll() {
    PageNode *temp = head;
    while (temp != NULL) {
        PageNode *next = temp->next;
        free(temp);
        temp = next;
    }
    head = current = NULL;
}

/* ---------- Main: menu-driven driver ---------- */
int main() {
    int choice;
    char url[100];

    while (1) {
        printf("\n===== Browser Navigation Menu =====\n");
        printf("1. Visit New Page\n");
        printf("2. Go Back\n");
        printf("3. Go Forward\n");
        printf("4. Display History\n");
        printf("5. Exit\n");
        printf("Enter your choice: ");
        scanf("%d", &choice);
        getchar();  /* consume trailing newline */

        switch (choice) {
            case 1:
                printf("Enter URL: ");
                fgets(url, sizeof(url), stdin);
                url[strcspn(url, "\n")] = '\0';
                visitPage(url);
                break;
            case 2:
                goBack();
                break;
            case 3:
                goForward();
                break;
            case 4:
                displayHistory();
                break;
            case 5:
                freeAll();
                printf("Exiting browser simulation.\n");
                exit(0);
            default:
                printf("Invalid choice! Try again.\n");
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

## 3.2 Step-by-Step Analytical Trace (Worked Example)

Consider this session trace to verify the algorithm:

| Step | User Action | State After Action | `current` URL | Forward Cleared? |
|------|-------------|--------------------|---------------|------------------|
| 1 | Visit `google.com` | `head→[google]←current` | google.com | No |
| 2 | Visit `wikipedia.org` | `google ⇄ wikipedia ←current` | wikipedia.org | No |
| 3 | Visit `github.com` | `google ⇄ wikipedia ⇄ github ←current` | github.com | No |
| 4 | Go Back | `google ⇄ wikipedia ←current ⇄ github` | wikipedia.org | No |
| 5 | Go Back | `google ←current ⇄ wikipedia ⇄ github` | google.com | No |
| 6 | Visit `stackoverflow.com` | `google ⇄ stackoverflow ←current` (github, wikipedia freed) | stackoverflow.com | **YES** |
| 7 | Go Forward | *No forward history* — print warning | stackoverflow.com | No |
| 8 | Go Back | `google ←current ⇄ stackoverflow` | google.com | No |

The pointer rewiring at **Step 6** is the most critical step:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{Before Step 6:} \quad & \text{google} \leftrightarrow \text{wikipedia} \leftarrow \text{current} \leftrightarrow \text{github} \\
\text{Free chain:} \quad & \text{free(wikipedia)}, \text{free(github)} \\
\text{After Step 6:} \quad & \text{google} \leftarrow \text{current} \leftrightarrow \text{stackoverflow}
\end{aligned}
$$

## 3.3 Time Complexity Derivation

For `visitPage`, the total time is bounded by two components:

$$
\begin{aligned}
T_{\text{visitPage}}(n, k) &= T_{\text{allocate}} + T_{\text{free forward}} + T_{\text{link}} \\
&= O(1) + O(k) + O(1) \\
&= O(1 + k) \\
&\le O(n) \quad \text{in worst case when } k = n - 1
\end{aligned}
$$

For `goBack` and `goForward`, the operation is a single pointer movement:

$$
T_{\text{goBack}} = T_{\text{goForward}} = O(1)
$$

For `displayHistory`, every node is visited exactly once:

$$
T_{\text{displayHistory}} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} O(1) = O(n)
$$

For the total memory footprint:

$$
S_{\text{total}} = n \times \big( \text{sizeof(char[100])} + 2 \times \text{sizeof(pointer)} \big) = O(n)
$$

> [!NOTE]
> **Why `O(k)` and not `O(1)` for visit?** Because when the user goes `A → B → C → B → D`, the forward chain `B → C` is destroyed, which requires iterating and freeing 1 node. In the worst case (going back to `head` and then visiting a new page), the entire forward chain must be freed. KTU exams expect this nuance.

