## 5.2 Part B — 14-Mark Questions (ESE Pattern with Internal Choice)

### **Question A (14 Marks)** `[KTU University Exam - Dec 2023]`

**(a)** Design and explain a C program to implement a dictionary that stores **word-meaning pairs** using a Binary Search Tree. The dictionary should support: insertion, search, and display (sorted). Show the BST node structure and briefly describe each operation's algorithm. **(7 marks)**

**(b)** Insert the following words into an **initially empty BST** in the given order. Show the BST after every insertion and display the in-order traversal: `Mango, Apple, Banana, Cherry, Apricot, Guava`. What property does the in-order traversal demonstrate? **(7 marks)**

---

#### 🔹 Model Solution for (a) — 7 Marks

**Node structure (2 marks):**
```c
typedef struct Node {
    char word[50];
    char meaning[200];
    struct Node *left, *right;
} Node;
```

**Insert Algorithm (2 marks):** Recursively compare using `strcmp`. If new word is lexicographically smaller, go left; if larger, go right; insert at the first NULL position.

**Search Algorithm (1.5 marks):** Recursively traverse using `strcmp` comparisons. Return the node on match or NULL on reaching a leaf.

**Display Algorithm (1.5 marks):** In-order traversal (`Left → Root → Right`) prints words in alphabetical order — the key property of using BST for a dictionary.

(Full C code is given in Section 3.4 above — examiners award partial marks for complete compilable code.)

