### **Question B (14 Marks — Alternative Choice)** `[KTU University Exam - May 2024]`

**(a)** Explain the three cases of **deleting a node** from a Binary Search Tree with suitable diagrams. Apply the delete operation to remove the word `Cherry` from the BST constructed in Part (b) of Question A, showing the tree after deletion. **(7 marks)**

**(b)** Differentiate between a **balanced BST** and a **skewed BST** in terms of height and search complexity. Suggest a real-world scenario where each would occur. **(7 marks)**

---

#### 🔹 Model Solution for (a) — 7 Marks

**Three deletion cases (4.5 marks):**

1. **Leaf node (no children):** Simply remove the node and set the parent's pointer to NULL. → `free(node); return NULL;`

2. **Node with one child:** Replace the node with its only child. → `parent->link = node->left (or right); free(node);`

3. **Node with two children:** Find the **in-order successor** (smallest node in right sub-tree) OR **in-order predecessor** (largest in left sub-tree). Copy its data into the target node, then recursively delete the successor/predecessor.

**Applying deletion of `Cherry` from the tree (2.5 marks):**

The tree before deletion:
```
           Mango
          /     \
      Apple   Cherry
          \       \
        Banana   Guava
        /
    Apricot
```

`Cherry` has **one child (Guava)** → Case 2 applies. Replace `Cherry` with `Guava`.

After deletion:
```
           Mango
          /     \
      Apple   Guava
          \
        Banana
        /
    Apricot
```

---

#### 🔹 Model Solution for (b) — 7 Marks

| Aspect | Balanced BST | Skewed BST |
|---|---|---|
| Definition | Height $h \approx \log_2 n$ | Height $h = n - 1$ |
| Search Time | $O(\log n)$ | $O(n)$ |
| Insertion Time | $O(\log n)$ | $O(n)$ |
| Tree Shape | Bushy, evenly distributed | Resembles a linked list |
| Space Utilization | Efficient | Wasted; pointer-heavy list |

**Real-world scenarios (4 marks):**

- **Balanced BST** scenario: Random-order word insertions into a spell-checker dictionary (e.g., user typing unpredictable words). The expected height is logarithmic.
- **Skewed BST** scenario: Loading an existing alphabetically-sorted wordlist (e.g., `a, b, c, d, ... z`) directly into a plain BST without rebalancing. Each new word becomes the right child of the previous one, producing a right-skewed tree with $O(n)$ search time.

> This is why self-balancing trees like **AVL** and **Red-Black Trees** are used in production — they guarantee $O(\log n)$ even for sorted input.

---

> [!WARNING]
> **🚨 KTU Examiner's Common Pitfall Callout — Where Students Lose Marks**
> 
> 1. **Forgetting the `getchar()`** after `scanf("%d", &choice)` — leaves a stray `\n` in the buffer, breaking `fgets()` for the word input. *(−1 to −2 marks in lab exams)*
> 2. **Using `=` instead of `strcmp`** in comparisons — `word < root->word` is **invalid for strings** in C. Always use `strcmp()` which returns `int`.
> 3. **Not handling the in-order successor case** in deletion — Case 3 (two children) is the most-missed sub-question in ESE.
> 4. **Memory leak** — forgetting to `free()` nodes on program exit (Case 3 deletion). Examiners check this in lab viva.
> 5. **Forgetting to update the `root` pointer** in `main()` after `insert` and `deleteNode` calls. The functions return the new root, and this must be assigned back.
> 6. **Not stripping the trailing newline** from `fgets()` — produces strings with a hidden `\n`, breaking lexicographic comparisons. Use `word[strcspn(word, "\n")] = 0;`.

